# The Tale of the First Shadow

In the beginning, when the world was young and bright, there lived a lonely Sun who blazed across an empty sky. The Sun shone with such brilliance that everything below was bathed in eternal light. There were no shadows, no darkness, no rest.

One day, the Sun looked down and saw a small child wandering the scorched earth, searching desperately for relief from the relentless brightness. The child's eyes were weary, and she stumbled as she walked.

Moved by compassion, the Sun realized that light alone was not a gift—it was a burden without balance. So the Sun did something it had never done before: it dimmed itself, just slightly, and called forth the Moon.

"Sister Moon," the Sun said, "I have learned that beauty requires contrast, and rest requires darkness. Will you share the sky with me?"

The Moon agreed, and as she rose, the first shadow fell across the earth. The child gasped in wonder and lay down in the cool darkness to rest.

From that day forward, the Sun and Moon took turns crossing the sky, creating day and night. And shadows became the gift that reminded all living things: even light needs darkness to be truly beautiful.
