# The Tale of the First Shadow

Long ago, when the world was young and the sun blazed eternally in the sky, there lived a curious child named Kael. The people of that time knew no darkness, no rest, and no dreams—only endless, burning light.

One day, Kael climbed the highest mountain to ask the sun why it never slept. The sun, touched by the child's bravery, answered: "I fear that if I close my eyes, the world will cease to exist."

Kael thought deeply and said, "But how can we know the value of your light if we never experience its absence?"

The sun paused, considering this wisdom from one so small. "You speak truth, little one. But I am afraid to leave you in nothingness."

"Then let me carry a piece of you," Kael offered, "so your light remains even when you rest."

Moved by this compassion, the sun agreed. It gave Kael a fragment of its essence, which became the first shadow—a gentle reminder of light even in darkness. From that day forward, the sun learned to set, giving birth to night, dreams, and the dance between shadow and light that continues still.
